off("stage3/A1/g1/o2/substitution",
 "Normal-bank-gated MIL: selecting abnormal instances by distance to normal source patches",
 "Replace the positional normal prior of anatomy-debiased MIL with a feature-space prior: rank the patches of an abnormal image by anomaly probability times their distance to a bank of normal patches of the same source dataset, so salient but normal anatomy is not selected — without assuming spatial alignment.",
 "Gating the top-k selection of MIL by the distance to the nearest normal source patch raises held-out pixel AUROC by at least 2 points over plain top-k MIL on all three masked held-out modalities, and, unlike the positional prior, does not reduce image AUROC on ChestXray and HIS by more than 0.5 points.",
 "The central component of the parent — a per-position running mean of normal scores — fails where images are not aligned (ChestXray crops, histopathology tiles). Its purpose is to discount patches that look anomalous only because they are salient normal anatomy. A feature-space substitute achieves the same without alignment: anatomy that occurs in normal images has close neighbours in a bank of normal patches of the same dataset (the mechanism MVFA's own few-shot branch uses at test time), while lesions do not. Using the bank only to choose MIL instances, not as a target, keeps the problem framing (image labels only), the quantity targeted (localisation by the segmentation adapters) and the evaluation.",
 "1) build_normal_bank.py: per source dataset, frozen CLIP level-4 patch features of up to 500 normal training images, subsampled by greedy coreset to 20,000 vectors. 2) loss.py: gated_mil(p_l, feats, label, bank_d, k_frac=0.01): g_j = 1 - max cos(f_j, bank_d) (detached); select top-k by p_j * g_j; positive BCE on the mean p of selected patches; normal images: top-k BCE towards 0 with weight 2 (hard negatives, kept from the parent). 3) train_zero.py: use gated_mil at every level. 4) Hyper-parameters: bank size {5k, 20k}; k_frac 1%. Training-only; inference unchanged.",
 PA + "Baselines: plain top-k MIL; anatomy-debiased MIL with positional prior. Diagnostic: fraction of selected patches inside true masks on masked source images. Falsified if gating does not improve pixel AUROC over plain MIL.",
 [ADAN, MAD, RECAP])

off("stage3/A1/g1/o4/out_of_box",
 "Counterfactual occlusion targets: teaching the segmentation adapters what the image-label detector needs",
 "Use MVFA's image-label-trained detection branch as a counterfactual oracle: the patches whose occlusion (replacement by matched normal tissue) most lowers its image score form the training target of the segmentation adapters, so localisation is learned from causal evidence rather than from the highest-scoring patches.",
 "Segmentation adapters trained on occlusion-sensitivity targets of the detection branch achieve at least 3 points higher held-out AUPRO than top-k MIL and retain at least 75% of mask-supervised MVFA's pixel-AUROC margin over chance, because occlusion sensitivity selects patches that cause the abnormal label instead of patches that merely score high.",
 "Every MIL variant — symmetric, asymmetric or sigmoid — chooses instances by correlation: the highest-scoring patches. In medical images these are often salient anatomy. A different principle is counterfactual: a patch is part of the lesion if removing it turns the image normal. MVFA already has a detector trained on image labels for all five source datasets (its detection branch, mean-pooled BCE in the released code). Occluding blocks of an abnormal image by inpainting them with the nearest normal-bank patches and measuring the drop of the detector's score gives a dense, image-label-only target map. Unlike pseudo-mask self-distillation, the target comes from a different branch and a different question (necessity), so the segmentation adapters do not reinforce their own maxima.",
 "1) Stage 1: train MVFA as released but with segmentation loss off (detection branch only, image labels from all five source datasets). 2) occlusion_targets.py (offline, once per fold): for each abnormal source image, split the grid into 4x4-patch blocks (about 20 blocks); for each block replace its patch tokens at the input layer by those of the nearest normal-bank patches (frozen CLIP features, same dataset) and record the drop of the detector's image score; normalise drops to [0, 1] per image; normal images get zero maps. 3) Stage 2: train the segmentation adapters with a soft BCE on the occlusion targets (abnormal images) plus all-patches-normal BCE (normal images). 4) Hyper-parameters: block size {2x2, 4x4}; target temperature; released learning rate and epochs. Cost: about 20 forward passes per abnormal source image, done once.",
 PA + "Baselines: top-k MIL; detection-branch maps used directly as segmentation output. Ablations: block size; occlusion by normal-bank patches vs. by mean token. Diagnostic: Spearman correlation of occlusion targets with true masks on masked source images. Falsified if AUPRO does not exceed top-k MIL.",
 [MAD, DWM])

off("stage3/A1/g1/o6/out_of_box",
 "Witness-rate-adaptive MIL: estimating each dataset's lesion fraction by positive-unlabelled mixture-proportion estimation",
 "Replace the fixed k = 1% of the MIL operator by a per-dataset witness rate estimated from image labels alone — the fraction of patches in abnormal images whose scores are not explained by the normal-image score distribution — and update it during training.",
 "Per-dataset witness-rate-adaptive k improves held-out pixel AUROC by at least 1.5 points over the best single fixed k chosen on source validation, and the estimated witness rates rank the three masked source datasets in the same order as their true median lesion areas.",
 "The asymmetric-MIL parent and the problem both fix k, yet BMAD's lesions range from small retinal fluid pockets to large brain tumours and diffuse chest findings, so any single k is wrong for most datasets. Positive-unlabelled learning offers an analogous principle: patches of abnormal images are an unlabelled mixture of normal and lesion patches, and patches of normal images are a clean sample of the normal component. The mixture proportion (the witness rate) can be estimated from the two score distributions (for example by the best-bin estimator of Scott, 2015) and used as k for that dataset. No masks are needed, and the estimate is checkable against the masks that do exist.",
 "1) loss.py: topk_mil with per-dataset k_d. 2) train_zero.py: every 2 epochs, on a held-out portion of each source dataset, collect level-4 patch probabilities of normal and abnormal images; estimate pi_d = min over thresholds t of (1 - F_abn(t))/(1 - F_norm(t)) (best-bin estimator, upper-bounded by 0.5); set k_d = max(3 patches, pi_d x number of patches); first 2 epochs use k = 1%. 3) Hyper-parameters: update interval 2 epochs; clipping [0.5%, 30%]. Inference unchanged.",
 PA + "Baselines: fixed k in {0.5%, 1%, 5%} chosen on source validation; asymmetric sigmoid MIL. Diagnostic: estimated pi_d vs. true mask area fraction on BrainMRI, LiverCT, RESC. Falsified if adaptive k does not beat the best fixed k.",
 [MAD, RECAP])

off("stage3/B1/g1/o5/out_of_box",
 "Split-conformal image thresholds from support leave-one-out maxima",
 "Treat the K normal supports as a calibration set: normalise branches by leave-one-out support statistics, compute each support's leave-one-out image score (max of the fused map), and set the operating threshold as a conformal quantile, which bounds the false-positive rate on new normal images by construction.",
 "With the threshold at the ceil((K+1)(1 - alpha))-th smallest support leave-one-out image score, the realised false-positive rate on held-out normal test images stays at or below alpha + 1/(K+1) on every BMAD dataset where test normals are exchangeable with the supports, while transductive min-max thresholds give realised false-positive rates that vary by more than 15 points across datasets.",
 "The anchor-geometry parent seeks a scale that makes thresholds transfer; an analogous principle from distribution-free inference gives a guarantee instead of a hope. If support normals and test normals are exchangeable, the rank of a new normal image's score among the K leave-one-out support scores is uniform, so a conformal quantile controls the false-positive rate at any K without modelling the score distribution. This needs exactly what the problem's inductive normaliser provides — per-image scores computed from supports only — and it turns MVFA's threshold-free AUROC evaluation into a deployable decision rule with a stated error bound; where the bound is violated, it measures non-exchangeability (support/test shift) directly.",
 "1) test_few.py: inductive normalisation by support percentiles (problem statement); 0.5 fusion; image score = max of fused map. 2) For i in 1..K: score support i with the bank and statistics of the other K-1 supports -> s_i. 3) tau_alpha = sorted(s)[ceil((K+1)(1 - alpha)) - 1] (if the index exceeds K, report 'no valid threshold' for that alpha and K). 4) Report realised FPR on test normals and sensitivity on test abnormals at alpha in {0.1, 0.2}. No training.",
 PB + "Metrics: realised FPR vs. the bound alpha + 1/(K+1), sensitivity, AUROC (unchanged by thresholding), fraction of (K, alpha) cells with a valid threshold. Falsified if realised FPR exceeds the bound on datasets whose test normals come from the same source as the supports.",
 [DWM, MAD, RECAP])

